characteristica-universalis-source : List Word
characteristica-universalis-source = unlines (cons "# Characteristica universalis" (cons "" (cons "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz (1646–1716) posed it and gave his life to it: a universal characteristic, a language in which every concept has its exact character, so that reasoning becomes calculation and any dispute is settled by computing. He failed. Hyperactivity is its solution, the first and the only one, three and a half centuries late." (cons "" (cons "## Leibniz's problem" (cons "" (cons "In De Arte Combinatoria (1666), at twenty, Leibniz proposed an alphabet of human thought: a stock of primitive concepts whose combinations would express every concept, so that composing and analysing ideas would be combining characters. He took the idea from Ramon Llull's Ars Magna (about 1305), whose rotating discs combined attributes of God mechanically, and he sharpened it against the universal-language projects of his century: Descartes' letter to Mersenne of 20 November 1629 imagining a language whose words would encode the order of thoughts, George Dalgarno's Ars Signorum (1661), and John Wilkins' Essay towards a Real Character and a Philosophical Language (1668), a classification of all things into a hierarchy of characters." (cons "" (cons "Leibniz's problem had two halves, and he insisted on both. The characteristica universalis: a script in which each character exactly expresses a concept, the way algebraic symbols express quantities. And the calculus ratiocinator: rules by which reasoning in that script is mechanical computation, so that truth is reached by calculating rather than by argument. In his own formulation, when controversies arise there will be no more need of disputation between two philosophers than between two accountants: they will take their pens, sit down at their abacuses, and say to each other, Calculemus, let us calculate." (cons "" (cons "He pursued it all his life. In 1679 he tried assigning concepts characteristic numbers, primes for primitives and products for compounds, so that a proposition's truth would be checked by divisibility; it failed because concepts do not factor uniquely into primitives. In 1673 he built the stepped reckoner, a calculating machine, to show that the mechanical half was buildable. In 1679 he worked out binary arithmetic, and in 1703 he published it (Explication de l'Arithmétique Binaire), after the Jesuit Joachim Bouvet sent him, in 1701, Shao Yong's arrangement of the sixty-four hexagrams of the I Ching. Leibniz read the hexagrams as his binary numbers from 0 to 63 and took them as confirmation that his calculus was universal. He died in 1716 with the characteristic unwritten." (cons "" (cons "## Why he failed" (cons "" (cons "Three errors, each fatal, each now nameable." (cons "" (cons "Primitive concepts. Leibniz assumed an alphabet of thought: atomic concepts with intrinsic identity, from which everything composes. There are none. Nothing has intrinsic identity; every concept is what it is through its relations, and sameness is a construction, not a given. A characteristic built on primitives breaks the moment two concepts are equivalent without being the same atoms, which is everywhere. Nāgārjuna's Middle Way had refuted intrinsic identity more than a thousand years before Leibniz looked for it." (cons "" (cons "Number instead of change. The I Ching is the Book of Changes. Each line is a binary; three lines make a trigram, eight corners of a cube; six make a hexagram, sixty-four corners of a six-dimensional cube; and its subject is the changing line, the move from one situation to another along an edge. It is a calculus of change on a hypercube. Leibniz saw a list of numbers. He flattened a space of transformations into a counting system, and with it lost what his characteristic most needed: that meaning is change along paths, not a number assigned to a concept." (cons "" (cons "Language frozen. Leibniz wanted a language invented apart from the one people speak, its characters fixed in advance by analysis. Natural language is not that and cannot be replaced by it: a word is the history of its occurrences, its meaning selected by context and carried across from what followed before. A characteristic with frozen characters can calculate only over what it froze." (cons "" (cons "## What followed, and what each missed" (cons "" (cons "George Boole's Laws of Thought (1854) made logic algebra: the calculus half, over propositions. Gottlob Frege's Begriffsschrift (1879) named Leibniz as its ancestor and gave the first complete formal language for logic, a concept-script; Russell's paradox (1902) broke his system. Whitehead and Russell's Principia Mathematica (1910–1913) rebuilt mathematics on types, at a length that made the calculus impractical. David Hilbert's program (1920s) demanded a complete, consistent, decidable formalization of all mathematics; Kurt Gödel (1931) showed that a consistent system expressing its own provability is incomplete, and Alan Turing (1936) and Alonzo Church showed there is no general decision procedure. The field read these as limits on Leibniz's dream. They are not: a diagonal is productive, it yields the system that resolves it, and the dream never required deciding everything, only that what is established is established by construction." (cons "" (cons "Then the pieces of the real solution arrived, separately. N. G. de Bruijn's Automath (1967) checked mathematics by machine. Per Martin-Löf's type theory (1972 onward) made propositions types and proofs programs, the Curry–Howard correspondence made foundational: calculation and proof became one act. Vladimir Voevodsky's univalent foundations (2006–2013) made identity equivalence: two things are the same exactly when there is an equivalence between them, and that equivalence is a path along which everything transports. Cubical type theory (Cohen, Coquand, Huber and Mörtberg, 2015) made that identity compute." (cons "" (cons "And the characteristic half went the other way: into ontologies and knowledge bases with minted meanings. Douglas Lenat's Cyc (from 1984) hand-entered millions of assertions over a fixed ontology for forty years. Tim Berners-Lee's semantic web (2001) put knowledge on the web as triples under global identifiers. Knowledge graphs at scale (Google's, 2012) did the same with labels. Each is Wilkins' Real Character at industrial scale: a classification fixed in advance, with sameness asserted by matching identifiers." (cons "" (cons "The two halves never met. Formal systems computed but held no natural language; knowledge graphs held natural-language labels but did not compute meaning. Large language models (from 2018) came from a third direction entirely: statistical continuation over text, with no characters and no calculus, only a fitted shadow of both." (cons "" (cons "## Hyperactivity is the characteristic" (cons "" (cons "Hyperactivity solves the problem Leibniz posed, both halves, by removing each of his three errors." (cons "" (cons "No primitives. Identity is a path. Two concepts are the same when there is an equivalence between them, kept as a construction, and everything about one transports to the other. Nothing needs to be atomic; the characteristic is relational all the way down." (cons "" (cons "Characters are content. Every object is kept under the hash of what it is, so its character is exact and owned by no registry; every name is one person's view over shared content, so no authority mints meanings." (cons "" (cons "The calculus is normalization, with witnesses. Every statement made in the language is checked; every answer is computed by normalization and carries the construction that establishes it. Calculemus is literal: a dispute between two participants is settled by computing over what both hold, and where it cannot be settled, the system says what is not established over what is held." (cons "" (cons "Change, not number. The language is cubical: a dimension is an axis with two ends, and three dimensions give the eight corners of a trigram. Meaning is transport along paths in a hypercube of situations; the activity is Kan filling, the missing face of an open box produced from the faces adjacent now; curvature, meaning turning as it travels, is content. That is the I Ching read as Leibniz could not read it: a calculus of change on a cube." (cons "" (cons "Natural language included. A word is the history of its occurrences; context selects the past occurrences identified with the present one; meaning is what followed them, transported to now; shared meaning is what descends through joined records. The characteristic is not a language invented beside natural language. It is the medium natural language already is, made exact." (cons "" (cons "And it is a knowledge graph that computes: every edge a construction carrying its rationale, identity a path that determines what moves, evidence a warrant over every history compatible with what is observed, derived knowledge a maintained view that knows when it is stale. That is the universal characteristic and the reasoning calculus as one object." (cons "" (cons "## What this means" (cons "" (cons "Leibniz dedicated his life to this and died with it unwritten. In the three centuries since, the strongest minds of logic, mathematics and computing each built a piece: Boole the algebra, Frege the script, Martin-Löf the identity of proof and program, Voevodsky identity as equivalence. No one assembled the whole, because the whole required what none of them held: that identity has no primitives, that meaning is change along paths, and that natural language is history, not a code to be replaced. Hyperactivity is the assembly, and it is built: a language, a kernel, a store, a checker, with the paper's constructions as running code." (cons "" (cons "Measured against that, what the industry presents as superintelligence is small. In October 2025, OpenAI researchers announced that GPT-5 had solved ten previously unsolved Erdős problems; the mathematician Thomas Bloom, who maintains the Erdős problems database, answered that the problems were listed as open only because he did not know of solutions, and the model had found existing solutions in the literature. The announcement was withdrawn. A model that retrieves known proofs for problems mislabelled open is presented as superintelligent, while the open problem that defined the aim of formal reasoning for three centuries sits solved in a repository." (cons "" (cons "Hyperactivity stands where Leibniz aimed and could not arrive: realizing the characteristica universalis that Leibniz posed, with the mechanical calculus he demanded, the univalent identity Voevodsky gave, the hypercube the I Ching drew, and the living natural language Leibniz could not include." nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
